Here is a basic implementation of the `renderFooterScripts` function in JavaScript, taking into consideration some of the realistic engineering scenarios you mentioned. The script fetching and rendering logic are separated from the main function to make it more modular and testable. Also, using localStorage for caching is used to prevent redundant requests for external scripts.

```javascript
// Helper function to fetch a script
async function fetchScript(src) {
  try {
    // Check if we have the content in cache
    const cachedContent = localStorage.getItem(src);
    if (cachedContent !== null) return cachedContent;

    const response = await fetch(src);

    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network error occurred');

    const content = await response.text();
    localStorage.setItem(src, content); // Cache the fetched script
    return content;
  } catch (error) {
    console.log('Error:', error);
    return '';
  }
}

// Helper function to render scripts into HTML
function renderScript({ src, code, type }) {
  if (!src && !code) throw new Error('Missing script source or inline code');

  let content = code;
  if (src) {
    // Fetch external script
    const fetchedContent = fetchScript(src);
    content = fetchedContent || '';
  }

  return `<script type="${type}">${content}</script>`;
}

// Main function to render footer scripts
async function renderFooterScripts(scripts) {
  if (!Array.isArray(scripts)) throw new Error('Expected an array of script objects');

  const rendered = await Promise.all(scripts.map(renderScript));

  return `<footer>${rendered.join('')}</footer>`;
}
```

Please note that the code assumes that you are running this in a modern JavaScript environment (like Node.js v14 or later, or a web browser). Also, inline scripts will not be cached, as they do not have an external source to fetch. Additionally, error handling is quite basic here and may need to be expanded depending on the specific requirements of your application.

```php
<?php
require_once 'config.php';
require_once 'DbConnection.php';
require_once 'ApiClient.php';
require_once 'RateLimiter.php';

class ClinicStatusUpdater {
    private $db;
    private $apiClient;
    private $rateLimiter;

    public function __construct(DbConnection $db, ApiClient $apiClient, RateLimiter $rateLimiter) {
        $this->db = $db;
        $this->apiClient = $apiClient;
        $this->rateLimiter = $rateLimiter;
    }

    public function updateClinicStatus($clinicId, $status, $ipAddress) {
        // Ensure the IP address is not rate limited
        if ($this->rateLimiter->isRateLimited($ipAddress)) {
            throw new Exception("You have made too many requests. Please try again later.");
        }

        // Start a database transaction
        $this->db->beginTransaction();

        try {
            $query = "SELECT status FROM clinics WHERE id = :id FOR UPDATE";
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare($query);
            $stmt->bindValue(':id', $clinicId, PDO::PARAM_INT);
            $stmt->execute();

            // Fetch the current status
            $currentStatus = $stmt->fetchColumn();

            if ($currentStatus === false) {
                throw new Exception("Clinic with ID: $clinicId does not exist");
            }

            // Update the clinic's status, last modified IP address, and last modified date
            $query = "UPDATE clinics 
                      SET status = :status, last_modified_ip = :ipAddress, last_modified_date = NOW() 
                      WHERE id = :id";
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare($query);
            $stmt->bindValue(':status', $status, PDO::PARAM_STR);
            $stmt->bindValue(':ipAddress', $ipAddress, PDO::PARAM_STR);
            $stmt->bindValue(':id', $clinicId, PDO::PARAM_INT);
            $stmt->execute();

            // Log the update action to another table in the database
            $query = "INSERT INTO clinic_logs (clinic_id, old_status, new_status) VALUES (:id, :oldStatus, :newStatus)";
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare($query);
            $stmt->bindValue(':id', $clinicId, PDO::PARAM_INT);
            $stmt->bindValue(':oldStatus', $currentStatus, PDO::PARAM_STR);
            $stmt->bindValue(':newStatus', $status, PDO::PARAM_STR);
            $stmt->execute();

            // Notify the external API service if the clinic's status changes to "closed" or "emergency"
            if ($currentStatus !== 'closed' && $currentStatus !== 'emergency' && 
                ($status === 'closed' || $status === 'emergency')) {
                $this->apiClient->notify($clinicId, $status);
            }

            // Commit the transaction
            $this->db->commit();
        } catch (Exception $e) {
            // Roll back the transaction if anything went wrong
            $this->db->rollBack();
            throw $e;  // Re-throw the exception to be caught at a higher level if necessary
        }
    }
}
?>
```
In this code, we're using prepared statements for each SQL query to prevent potential SQL injection. The database connection is managed by `DbConnection` class which abstracts away PDO details and makes the application more modular and testable. We also have a separate rate limiter class that keeps track of requests from individual IP addresses to ensure that the same user doesn't make too many requests in a short period. An API client is used to notify external services if necessary, this abstracts away the actual network calls making it easier to switch out with another implementation or mock for testing. Error handling and logging are also omitted for simplicity but should be added in a real-world application.

