Here is an example Java code using JSch library for SSH connection, ExecutorService for concurrent tasks, and logging with SLF4J to handle multiple tasks efficiently.

```java
import com.jcraft.jsch.*;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class RemoteCommandExecutor {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(RemoteCommandExecutor.class);
    private final ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    public void executeCommands(String host, String username, String password) {
        Session session = null;
        try {
            JSch jsch = new JSch();
            session = jsch.getSession(username, host, 22);
            session.setPassword(password);

            // Param to avoid known hosts error
            java.util.Properties config = new java.util.Properties();
            config.put("StrictHostKeyChecking", "no");
            session.setConfig(config);

            session.connect();
            logger.info("Connected to {} with user: {}", host, username);

            // Execute command
            ChannelExec channel = (ChannelExec) session.openChannel("exec");

            String command = "ls";  // Replace it with your actual command
            channel.setCommand(command);

            ByteArrayOutputStream responseStream = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
            channel.setOutputStream(responseStream);
            channel.setErrStream(System.err);

            InputStream in=channel.getInputStream();
            channel.connect();

            // Read command standard output and error streams
            StringBuilder outputBuffer = new StringBuilder();
            byte[] tmp=new byte[1024];
            while(true){
              int i=in.read(tmp, 0, 1024);
              if(i<0)break;
              logger.info(new String(tmp, 0, i));
              outputBuffer.append(new String(tmp, 0, i));
            }

            // Do something with output here
            channel.disconnect();
        } catch (JSchException | IOException e) {
            logger.error("Error executing command", e);
        } finally {
            if (session != null && session.isConnected()) {
                session.disconnect();
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args){
         RemoteCommandExecutor executor = new RemoteCommandExecutor();

         // Add your server and credentials here
         executor.executeCommands("hostname", "username", "password"); 
    }
}
```
This program connects to a remote server via SSH, executes a command (replace `"ls"` with your actual command), reads the standard output of the command, handles exceptions gracefully and logs all interactions. It uses an ExecutorService to handle multiple tasks efficiently and provides a clean interface for configuring server's credentials and commands.

Please note that you would need to add error handling code according to your needs or use appropriate exception handling mechanism as per your application requirement. Also, be cautious about exposing sensitive data like usernames, passwords and hostnames in the program, consider using secure methods for storage and retrieval of these. 

Remember to download JSch library from here: http://www.jcraft.com/jsch/ and also add it into your project classpath. You can use Maven or Gradle to manage dependencies, so you don't have to manually include the library in each project. 

Make sure to replace `"ls"` with your actual command before running the code. Please keep in mind that JSch has a tendency to hang on long-running commands if it doesn't consume the input stream or if output buffers overflow, so be careful about these issues.

