Here is a basic example of how you could implement this kind of functionality in C#. This implementation does not handle any encryption algorithm besides AES-CBC as your problem was asking for dynamic encryption keys and IVs. For the GCM mode you need to use the AesGcm class, which isn't demonstrated here because it uses a tag for checking integrity that makes its usage more complex than CBC.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

public static class AESEncryption
{
    public static byte[] Encrypt(byte[] data, byte[] key, byte[] iv)
    {
        using (var aes = new AesCryptoServiceProvider())
        {
            aes.KeySize = 256; // in bits
            aes.BlockSize = 128; // in bits
            aes.Mode = CipherMode.CBC; 
            aes.Padding = PaddingMode.PKCS7;

            aes.Key = key;
            aes.IV = iv;

            using (var encryptor = aes.CreateEncryptor(aes.Key, aes.IV))
            {
                return PerformCryptography(data, encryptor);
            }
        }
    }

    public static byte[] Decrypt(byte[] encryptedData, byte[] key, byte[] iv)
    {
        using (var aes = new AesCryptoServiceProvider())
        {
            aes.KeySize = 256; // in bits
            aes.BlockSize = 128; // in bits
            aes.Mode = CipherMode.CBC; 
            aes.Padding = PaddingMode.PKCS7;

            aes.Key = key;
            aes.IV = iv;

            using (var decryptor = aes.CreateDecryptor(aes.Key, aes.IV))
            {
                return PerformCryptography(encryptedData, decryptor);
            }
        }
    }

    private static byte[] PerformCryptography(byte[] data, ICryptoTransform cryptoTransform)
    {
        using (var ms = new MemoryStream())
        {
            using (var cryptoStream = new CryptoStream(ms, cryptoTransform, CryptoStreamMode.Write))
            {
                cryptoStream.Write(data, 0, data.Length);
                cryptoStream.FlushFinalBlock();

                return ms.ToArray();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This is a very simple example and doesn't include any kind of key or IV management beyond simply accepting them as parameters. To make this class work in an existing application, you would need to modify it to handle things like loading keys from a secure location, generating new keys on demand, etc., and then integrate it into your application framework appropriately.

It also doesn't include any kind of error handling or documentation which should be included for production quality code. Also, it doesn't scale very well under high concurrency because the AesCryptoServiceProvider class isn't thread-safe, so you need to handle that situation with care in a multi-threaded environment.

