Here is a simple solution in Java using Jackson library:

```Java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import java.util.*;

public class JsonExtractor {
    private ObjectMapper objectMapper = new ObjectMapper();
    private Map<String, List<JsonNode>> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public List<JsonNode> extractData(String json, String path) throws Exception {
        if (cache.containsKey(path)) {
            return cache.get(path);
        } else {
            JsonNode rootNode = objectMapper.readTree(json);
            Iterator<JsonNode> nodes = rootNode.at(path).elements();

            List<JsonNode> dataList = new ArrayList<>();
            while (nodes.hasNext()) {
                JsonNode node = nodes.next();

                if (!node.isMissingNode() && !node.isNull()) {
                    dataList.add(node);
                } else {
                    throw new Exception("No data found at the specified path: " + path);
                }
            }

            cache.put(path, dataList);

            return dataList;
        }
    }
}
```

This `JsonExtractor` class provides a method `extractData()` which parses and extracts JSON data at the specified path using Jackson's `ObjectMapper` class. The extracted data is stored in a cache to improve performance when the same JSON paths are queried repeatedly. 

The function throws an exception if no data is found at the given path or if multiple nodes match, indicating that the provided JSON path does not uniquely identify a single node. To handle exceptions gracefully and provide clear error messages, catch any `Exception`s thrown by Jackson's methods and wrap them into your own `Exception` with custom message.

However, please note that Jackson is designed to work efficiently with large JSON documents. If you find performance issues when dealing with very large JSON structures, consider breaking down the document into smaller parts or using streaming APIs provided by Jackson for more efficient memory usage and lower garbage collection overhead. The impact of different JSON document sizes on this method's performance depends primarily on the complexity and depth of the path string that is passed to extract data from a given JSON structure.

