Here is an example of how you could write such a function in C++ using Java's serialization framework. Note that this implementation will only work on systems where Java serialization is supported, as the deserialization process depends on JNI (Java Native Interface) calls which are specific to each platform. It also assumes that the required classes exist in the system's classpath and have been registered for use with Java serialization.

```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>
#include "jni.h"

template<typename T>
T deserializeFromFile(const std::string& filename) {
    // Check if the class exists in the system's classpath
    jclass cls = env->FindClass("com/example/MyClass");
    if (cls == nullptr) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Class not found in classpath.");
    }

    // Load resource bundle from file
    std::ifstream is(filename, std::ios::binary);
    if (!is.good()) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to open file: " + filename);
    }

    // Count items in the bundle
    int itemCount = ...;  // Implementation depends on how you're storing these

    // Read resource bundle data as byte array
    char* buffer = new char[itemCount * sizeof(T)];  // Allocate memory for bundle
    is.read(buffer, itemCount * sizeof(T));

    if (is.fail()) {
        delete[] buffer;
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to read file: " + filename);
    }

    // Attempt to deserialize it into an object of the specified class
    jbyteArray byteArray = env->NewByteArray(itemCount * sizeof(T));
    if (env->ExceptionCheck()) {
        delete[] buffer;
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to create JNI byte array.");
    }

    // Attempt to deserialize the data
    env->SetByteArrayRegion(byteArray, 0, itemCount * sizeof(T), reinterpret_cast<jbyte*>(buffer));
    if (env->ExceptionCheck()) {
        delete[] buffer;
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to set byte array region.");
    }

    jmethodID mid = env->GetStaticMethodID(cls, "deserialize", "([B)Ljava/lang/Object;");
    if (mid == nullptr || env->ExceptionCheck()) {
        delete[] buffer;
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to get deserialization method.");
    }

    jobject object = env->CallStaticObjectMethod(cls, mid, byteArray);
    if (env->ExceptionCheck() || object == nullptr) {
        delete[] buffer;
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to deserialize object from file: " + filename);
    }

    // Release resources and return the object
    delete[] buffer;
    env->DeleteLocalRef(byteArray);

    // Validate integrity of serialized file...

    return reinterpret_cast<T>(object);
}
```
This is a simplified example and may need to be adjusted based on the specifics of your project. For instance, you would need to implement a method for validating the integrity of the serialized data. Additionally, this function assumes that the `deserialize` method is static and takes in a byte array as an argument. You might also want to consider adding error checking code after each JNI call to check for exceptions.

Here's an outline of how you might write this function in Rust using the `std::fs`, `rayon` for parallel processing, `log` for logging and `rustc_codegen_llvm` and `inkwell` for LLVM IR generation and optimization. 

Please note that writing code for a directory requires extensive error handling and testing because it's not always possible to compile all Rust files in the directory, so you need to handle such scenarios properly. This example also doesn't include logging integration as it is highly dependent on your exact use case.

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use rayon::prelude::*;
use log::{info, error};
use rustc_codegen_llvm::backend::BackEnd;
use inkwell::builder::BuilderContext;
use std::io::Write;

fn generate_ir(dir: &str, logger: &log::Logger) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let dir = PathBuf::from(dir);

    if !dir.is_dir() {
        return Err("Given path is not a directory".into());
    }

    let files = fs::read_dir(&dir)?
                  .filter_map(Result::ok)
                  .filter(|entry| entry.path().extension().unwrap_or_default() == "rs")
                  .collect::<Vec<_>>();

    info(logger, "Started processing files");

    let b = BuilderContext::new();

    files.into_par_iter().for_each(|file| {
        match std::process::Command::new("rustc")
               .arg(&format_args(vec![
                   "--emit", "llvm-ir", "-C", "opt-level=3", "-o", file.path()], 
                   |x| format_args(&["-C", "llvm-passes"], |y| y.to_string())
               ))
               .status() {
            Ok(_) => info(logger, &format_args(&[file.path().display().to_string(), "Compilation Succeeded"], |x| x.into())),
            Err(e) =>  error(logger, &format_args(&["Failed to compile", file.path().display().to_string()], |x| x.into()))
        }
    });

    info(logger, "Finished processing files");

    Ok(())
}
```
Please note that this code uses `std::process::Command` to call `rustc`, but it's not really a robust way of doing things. You should use rustc_driver crate which provides a more Rust-friendly interface for using rustc as a library. 

Also LLVM passes configuration is not included in this example because the rustc_codegen_llvm and inkwell APIs do not expose a way to pass optimization passes to LLVM IR generator. This feature is available through the C bindings of LLVM, but it's not easy to use from Rust with no experience on how exactly those should be used.

