For example, here's a simple OpenSSL-based command line tool that uses the DES-CBC cipher with a specified key and initialization vector:
```c
#include <openssl/conf.h>
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <openssl/err.h>

void handleErrors(void)
{
    ERR_print_errors_fp(stderr);
    abort();
}

int encrypt(unsigned char *plaintext, int plaintext_len, unsigned char *key,
            unsigned char *iv, unsigned char *ciphertext)
{
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX *ctx;

    int len;

    int ciphertext_len;

    if(!(ctx = EVP_CIPHER_CTX_new()))
        handleErrors();

    if(1 != EVP_EncryptInit_ex(ctx, EVP_des_cbc(), NULL, key, iv))
        handleErrors();

    if(1 != EVP_EncryptUpdate(ctx, ciphertext, &len, plaintext, plaintext_len))
        handleErrors();
    ciphertext_len = len;

    if(1 != EVP_EncryptFinal_ex(ctx, ciphertext + len, &len)) 
        handleErrors();
    ciphertext_len += len;

    EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free(ctx);

    return ciphertext_len;
}
```
Please note that handling multiple modes of operation and configuring IVs will require different sets of code, each with their own potential issues. You might also want to consider using a modern cryptography library like Libsodium or BoringSSL instead. These libraries offer an API that abstracts many of these details away and provides better security properties.

