Here is a Rust solution for your problem with some assumptions and simplifications:

- Assumption: Your environment variable `DIR_PATH` will be stored in a file called ".env" which should contain only one line like `DIR_PATH=/path/to/directory`.
- Simplification: We're not doing any thread synchronization here, as Rust handles concurrent access to shared memory safely by default. If you need more complex synchronization (e.g., mutual exclusion), you may want to look at the `std::sync` crate and its primitives for that.
- Simplification: We're not handling file system errors, as Rust provides many of them with detailed messages.
- Simplification: Logging is simply writing to stdout here. You might prefer to use a dedicated logging library (like the `log` crate).

```rust
use chrono::{DateTime, Utc};
use std::env;
use std::fs;
use std::path::PathBuf;

fn create_directory(dir_name: &str) {
    let env_var = "DIR_PATH";
    let mut dir_path = match env::var(env_var) {
        Ok(path) => PathBuf::from(path),
        Err(_) => {
            printlnerror: Failed to read environment variable {}", env_var);
            return;
        }
    };

    let timestamp = Utc::now().timestamp();
    dir_path.push(&formatcalled!"{dir_name}_{timestamp}");

    match fs::create_dir(&dir_path) {
        Err(error) => printlnerror: Failed to create directory {}", error),
        Ok(_) => printlninfo: Created directory {}", dir_path.display()),
    };

    env::set_var(env_var, dir_path.as_path().to_str().unwrap());

    if let Err(error) = fs::remove_dir(&formatcalled!"{dir_name}_{timestamp}") {
        printlnerror: Failed to remove temporary directory {}", error);
    }
}
```
This code assumes that the timestamp is unique for each run, which may not be the case if your program runs very quickly (within a second). If this is a problem, you might want to use something like UUIDs or some other kind of globally unique identifier. 

