Here's a very basic example of how you could accomplish this task using boost::asio for the networking layer, boost::beast for WebSocket functionality and OpenSSL for TLS/SSL operations. 

This code will only work if your system has libssl-dev installed which includes OpenSSL header files. You can install it on Ubuntu or Debian based systems with sudo apt install libssl-dev.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <boost/beast.hpp>
#include <openssl/ssl.h>

namespace beast = boost::beast;         // from <boost/beast.hpp>
namespace http = beast::http;           // from <boost/beast/http.hpp>
namespace websocket = beast::websocket; // from <boost/beast/websocket.hpp>
namespace net = boost::asio;            // from <boost/asio.hpp>
using tcp = net::ip::tcp;               // from <boost/asio/ip/tcp.hpp>

class WebSocketTlsClient {
public:
    WebSocketTlsClient(net::io_context& ioc, ssl::context& ctx) 
        : resolver_(net::make_strand(ioc)), ws_(net::make_strand(ioc), ctx){}

    ~WebSocketTlsClient() { Close(); }

    void Connect(const std::string& host, const std::string& port) {
        auto const results = resolver_.resolve(host, port);
        net::async_connect(ws_.next_layer(), results.begin(), results.end(), 
            beast::bind_front_handler(&WebSocketTlsClient::OnConnect, shared_from_this()));
    }

    void Send(const std::string& message) {
        ws_.async_write(net::buffer(message), 
            beast::bind_front_handler(&WebSocketTlsClient::OnWrite, shared_from_this()));
    }

    void Close() {
        ws_.async_close(websocket::close_code::none, 
            beast::bind_front_handler(&WebSocketTlsClient::OnClose, shared_from_this()));
    }

private:
    tcp::resolver resolver_;
    websocket::stream<beast::ssl_stream<tcp::socket>> ws_;
};
``` 
This is a very basic example and does not include the actual TLS/SSL setup, WebSocket handshake or any of the other necessary steps. For a full implementation you'd need to work with more complex examples available online (like those provided by Boost.Beast's official documentation). This code is just a starting point.

This task requires knowledge of SQL databases, dynamic memory allocation, error handling, thread safety, optimization techniques, testing, scalability, and extensibility. Below is a sample implementation in C using pthread library for synchronization mechanisms and libpq library for PostgreSQL connection. Note that this code does not compile without additional libraries (such as sqlite3 or pg), and the logging mechanism is just a printf statement for simplicity:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include "libpq-fe.h" // libpq library for PostgreSQL connection
#include <pthread.h> 

typedef struct Record {
    int key;
    long timestamp;
    char* description;
} Record;

PGconn *connectDB() {
    PGconn *conn = PQconnectdb("host=localhost dbname=test user=postgres password=secret");

    if (PQstatus(conn) != CONNECTION_OK) {
        printf("Database connection failed: %s", PQerrorMessage(conn));
        exit(0);
    }

    return conn;
}

int getRecordCount() {
    PGresult *res = PQexec(connectDB(), "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM records");
    int record_count = atoi(PQgetvalue(res, 0, 0));

    PQclear(res);

    return record_count;
}

void* initializeRecords() {
    pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; // mutex for synchronization

    int record_count = getRecordCount();

    srand(time(0)); 

    Record* records = malloc(record_count * sizeof(Record));

    if (records == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.");
        exit(0);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < record_count; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // lock to ensure thread safety

        records[i].key = rand() % 10001;
        records[i].timestamp = time(NULL) + (rand() % (31536000 + 1)); // random timestamp within current time and 1 year ahead
        records[i].description = malloc(256 * sizeof(char));

        if (records[i].description == NULL) {
            printf("Memory allocation failed.");
            exit(0);
        }

        strncpy(records[i].description, "Random description", 256); // set a random description

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // unlock after modifying records to allow other threads to access
    }

    return (void*) records;
}
```

The above function `initializeRecords` can be called to initialize the records from an SQL database. In a production environment, you'd want to call this in some sort of main loop that can handle failures and retries when connecting to the database or memory allocation fails. You could also implement additional features such as error handling for invalid data types and adding logging mechanisms. 

This code does not include testing as it will depend on your specific use case, but you could create a separate test function to ensure all methods are working correctly under various conditions. For scalability, this would likely involve optimizing the database queries used in `getRecordCount` and `initializeRecords` to efficiently handle large datasets.

