This task requires extensive JavaScript knowledge, and the implementation of such functionality is beyond the scope of this platform. However, here is a general guideline for creating such a module:

```javascript
class DynamicLoader {
    constructor() {
        this.loaded = [];
        this.failedFiles = new Set();
    }

    load(files) {
        const promises = files.map((file, index) => this._loadFile(file, index));
        return Promise.allSettled(promises);
    }

    _loadFile(url, index) {
        if (this.loaded[index]) return Promise.resolve();

        return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
            const script = document.createElement('script');
            script.src = url;

            script.onerror = () => {
                this.failedFiles.add(url);
                reject(`Failed to load file: ${url}`);
            };

            script.onload = () => {
                // Check if the loaded file is dependent on any other files
                const dependencies = window.__dependencyMap[url]; 

                if (dependencies) {
                    this._loadFile(dependencies).then(() => resolve());
                } else {
                    resolve();
                }
            };

            document.head.appendChild(script);
        });
    }
}
```
In the above code:
- The `DynamicLoader` class handles dynamic loading of JavaScript files with dependencies and error handling mechanisms. 
- `load` function takes in an array of file URLs, creates a promise for each file and returns a Promise.allSettled() which resolves when all promises are either fulfilled or rejected.
- `_loadFile` is the private method used by load to load individual files using dynamic script tag injection. It handles error events and loads dependent files if any exist. 

