Implementing cryptographic algorithms in C requires a deep understanding of computer science principles and cryptography. Here is a simple example on how you can begin doing this for the DES algorithm. This sample does not include all the functionalities described in your question due to space restrictions, but it should give you an idea about what needs to be done. Note that this code doesn't include any error handling, key derivation functions, or security checks to ensure robustness of operations and it also lacks unit tests and secure memory management strategies for performance optimization and scalability.

```C
#include <openssl/des.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void des_encrypt(DES_cblock key, DES_cblock ivec, const char* input, char* output) {
    DES_key_schedule ks;
    DES_set_key(&key, &ks);

    // allocate cipher block (ECB mode doesn't use an IV)
    DES_cblock in = {};
    DES_cblock out = {};

    memcpy(in, input, 8);

    DES_cbc_encrypt(&in, &out, 1, &ks, &ivec, DES_ENCRYPT);

    memcpy(output, &out, 8);
}

void des_decrypt(DES_cblock key, DES_cblock ivec, const char* input, char* output) {
    DES_key_schedule ks;
    DES_set_key(&key, &ks);

    // allocate cipher block (ECB mode doesn't use an IV)
    DES_cblock in = {};
    DES_cblock out = {};

    memcpy(in, input, 8);

    DES_cbc_encrypt(&in, &out, 1, &ks, &ivec, DES_DECRYPT);

    memcpy(output, &out, 8);
}

int main() {
    const char* plaintext = "helloooo"; // make sure this is always 8 bytes or less
    DES_cblock key = {'1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6', '7', '8'};
    DES_cblock ivec = {'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e', 'f', 'g', 'h'};

    char encrypted[9] = {0};
    des_encrypt(key, ivec, plaintext, encrypted);

    char decrypted[9] = {0};
    des_decrypt(key, ivec, encrypted, decrypted);

    printf("Original: %s\n", plaintext);
    printf("Encrypted: ");
    for (int i=0;i<8;++i)
        printf("%X ", encrypted[i]);
    printf("\n");
    printf("Decrypted: %s\n", decrypted);

    return 0;
}
```
To compile this code, you'll need to install the OpenSSL library and then compile with `-lcrypto` flag. For example: `gcc des_cbc.c -o des_cbc -lcrypto`. Also keep in mind that DES algorithm is considered weak nowadays due to its short key length (56 bits) and should not be used for security-critical applications.

