Here is a simple PHP function for authenticating a user using OAuth2.0:

```php
<?php
function authenticateUser($clientId, $clientSecret) {
    // Initialize CURL:
    $ch = curl_init('https://oauth.example.com/token');

    // Create an array of post fields:
    $postFields = [
        'grant_type' => 'client_credentials',
        'client_id' => $clientId,
        'client_secret' => $clientSecret
    ];

    // Set the url, number of POST vars, POST data:
    curl_setopt($ch,CURLOPT_POST, true);
    curl_setopt($ch,CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, $postFields);

    // So that curl_exec returns the contents of the cURL; rather than echoing it:
    curl_setopt($ch,CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true); 

    // Execute post and store result in $response:
    $response = curl_exec($ch);

    // Close cURL resource, free up system resources:
    curl_close($ch);

    // Decode JSON response:
    $tokenData = json_decode($response);

    if (isset($tokenData->access_token)) {
        // Store the access token in a session variable
        $_SESSION['access_token'] = $tokenData->access_token;
        return true;
    } else {
        throw new Exception('Failed to authenticate user');
    }
}
?>
```
This is just a simple example of how you might start to handle authentication using OAuth2.0 in PHP. The actual implementation will depend on the specific requirements and constraints of your application. Please refer to PHP documentation for more information regarding curl library, session handling and JSON decoding.

