For the forward list modification task, you will have to use C++ as well as C has no built-in support for lists. Here's a simple example of how this could be implemented in C++:

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>

void insertAfter(std::forward_list<std::string>& flist, const std::string& search, const std::string& insertion) {
    auto prev = flist.before_begin();
    for (auto it = flist.begin(); it != flist.end(); ++it, ++prev) {
        if (*it == search) {
            flist.insert_after(it, insertion);
            return;
        }
    }

    // If we're here, the search string was not found in the list, so append the insertion at the end
    flist.insert_after(prev, insertion);
}
``` 

In this function, `it` is a forward iterator that points to each element of the list in order, and `prev` is an iterator that always points "one before" `it` (or at the beginning of the list if `it == flist.begin()`). We use `flist.insert_after(it, insertion)` to insert a new element after the current one, effectively creating a new element between the two existing elements.

Here is a C++ implementation that uses std::forward_list to store strings and provides thread safety using mutexes. It also supports an option to enable or disable dynamic resizing of the forward list. 

Please note, this code will throw exceptions when a mutex fails to lock due to a system error, so you need to handle them in your main program. Also, you would need to implement a way to integrate with an external data source as it is not defined in this function. 

```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <mutex>
#include <stdexcept>

class StringForwardList {
private:
    std::forward_list<std::string> list;
    bool dynamicResizeEnabled = true;
    std::recursive_mutex mtx; // recursive_mutex allows same thread to lock multiple times.

public:
    void update(const std::string& search, const std::string& update) {
        std::lock_guard<std::recursive_mutex> guard(mtx);

        auto it = list.begin();
        while (it != list.end()) {
            if (*it == search) {
                it = list.insert_after(it, update); // insert after search string
                ++it; // skip over the inserted element to prevent infinite loop
                return;
            }
            ++it;
        }

        // if search is not found, append at end of list
        list.push_front(update);
    }

    void enableDynamicResize() {
        std::lock_guard<std::recursive_mutex> guard(mtx);
        dynamicResizeEnabled = true;
    }

    void disableDynamicResize() {
        std::lock_guard<std::recursive_mutex> guard(mtx);

        if (list.empty()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("List is empty");
        }

        dynamicResizeEnabled = false;
    }
};
``` 
In this code, update() function accepts a search string and an update string. It finds the first occurrence of the search string in the list and inserts the update string after it. If the search string is not found, the update string will be appended to the end of the list. The enableDynamicResize() and disableDynamicResize() methods control whether dynamic resizing is enabled or disabled respectively.

