Here is an example of such a function. It uses a queue-based approach to handle promises in order to manage resources efficiently and avoid overwhelming the API service. If an API call fails or times out, it will automatically be retried until a maximum retry limit is reached. The results are gathered using reduce() and any errors encountered during this process are collected and returned for later analysis:

```javascript
async function asyncReduce(arr, initialValue, callback) {
    let accumulator = initialValue;
    const queue = [];
    const errors = [];

    arr.forEach((val, index) => {
      // Simulating multiple sources using Math.random()
      const sourceId = Math.floor(Math.random() * 10);

      const promiseCallback = () => 
        fetchExternalAPI(sourceId, val)
          .then((data) => callback(accumulator, data))
          .catch((error) => errors.push({ index, error }));

      queue.push(promiseCallback);
    });

    for (const promise of queue) {
      accumulator = await promise();
    }

    return { result: accumulator, errors };
}
```
To handle the external API service call and implement a rate limit mechanism, we can use exponential backoff with jitter as follows:

```javascript
let failureCounts = {};
const MAX_RETRIES = 5;
const BASE_DELAY = 1000; // 1 second
const MAX_JITTER = 400;  // +- 400ms of jitter

async function fetchExternalAPI(sourceId, value) {
    if (failureCounts[sourceId] >= MAX_RETRIES) {
        throw new Error('Max retries reached');
    }

    let delay = Math.pow(2, failureCounts[sourceId]) * BASE_DELAY;
    delay += Math.random() * MAX_JITTER - (MAX_JITTER / 2); // +- jitter

    try {
        const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/data?sourceId=${sourceId}&value=${value}`);

        if (!response.ok) {
            failureCounts[sourceId]++;
            throw new Error('Request failed');
        }

        const data = await response.json();
        failureCounts[sourceId] = 0; // reset counter on success

        return data;
    } catch (error) {
        if (failureCounts[sourceId] < MAX_RETRIES) {
            setTimeout(fetchExternalAPI, delay);
        } else {
            throw error;
        }
    }
}
```
This is a simple and effective solution to the problem. However, it's important to note that handling async code this way can get complicated quickly if you have a lot of asynchronous operations running at once or if rate limiting is an issue in your API service. In such cases, consider using libraries like async or bluebird for better control over concurrency and error handling, or use dedicated task scheduling tools/services.

