Here is a basic example using POSIX threads (pthreads) for concurrency and error checking as per your request. Note that this doesn't involve an external data source, but instead reads from stdin. This code is written in C and is not intended to be run on its own; it simply provides a starting point for your custom application:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <assert.h>

int largest = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void *readAndProcess(void *arg) {
    int input;
    while (scanf("%d", &input) == 1) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        if (input > largest) largest = input;
        printf("Largest number so far: %d\n", largest);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;  // signals that the thread has finished
}

int main() {
    pthread_t thread;
    int error = pthread_create(&thread, NULL, &readAndProcess, NULL);
    assert(error == 0);

    pthread_join(thread, NULL);  // wait for the thread to finish

    printf("Final largest number: %d\n", largest);
    return 0;
}
```
This program reads integers from stdin in a separate thread. The 'readAndProcess' function is called concurrently by the created thread, reading an integer each time and updating 'largest' if it encounters a larger number. Note that the mutex lock protects access to 'largest', ensuring that no race conditions occur due to simultaneous access from multiple threads.

This code doesn't include error handling for malformed input data or disconnection from the data source, as those are more specific issues and would require additional logic, possibly including signaling when a read fails and retrying with exponential backoff until successful. 

