This problem is quite complex and requires multiple files, but I can provide you with a basic idea of how to start it using C programming language. Keep in mind that this is just a mock up example and doesn't cover everything mentioned in the question. Also, semaphores in C are not high-performance or fault-tolerant by themselves, for that you might want to use some form of interprocess communication (IPC) such as shared memory with semaphores.

Firstly let's start with a semaphore management system:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct Sem {
    char name[256];
    sem_t sem;
} Sem;

Sem sems[100];
int numSems = 0;
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void createSem(char* name) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    strcpy(sems[numSems].name, name);
    sem_init(&sems[numSems].sem, 0, 1);
    numSems++;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

void deleteSem(char* name) {
    for (int i = 0; i < numSems; ++i) {
        if (strcmp(sems[i].name, name) == 0) {
            sem_destroy(&sems[i].sem);
            memmove(&sems[i], &sems[i + 1], (numSems - i - 1) * sizeof(Sem));
            numSems--;
            break;
        }
    }
}

void P(char* name) {
    for (int i = 0; i < numSems; ++i) {
        if (strcmp(sems[i].name, name) == 0) {
            sem_wait(&sems[i].sem);
