```javascript
function asyncReduceArray(array, initialValue, callback) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    let accumulator = initialValue;
    const reduceHelper = (index = 0) => {
      if (index < array.length) {
        setImmediate(() => {
          try {
            accumulator = callback(accumulator, array[index]);
            reduceHelper(index + 1);
          } catch (error) {
            reject(`Error in reduce operation: ${error}`);
          }
        });
      } else {
        resolve(accumulator);
      }
    };
    reduceHelper();
  });
}

// Test the function
const testArray = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
asyncReduceArray(testArray, 0, (a, b) => a + b)
  .then((result) => console.log('Result:', result)) // should print "Result: 15"
  .catch((error) => console.error('Error:', error));
```

The above code defines an `asyncReduceArray` function that takes an array, an initial value, and a callback to be applied on each element of the array. It creates a promise which resolves with the final accumulated result when all elements have been processed. This is done asynchronously using `setImmediate()`.

