For JavaScript part, a simple implementation of asyncArrayReduce function can be done using promise and setTimeout for throttling:

```javascript
async function asyncArrayReduce(array, initialValue, accumulatorFn, config) {
    let result = initialValue;
    const semaphore = new Semaphore(config.maxParallelOps);

    for (let i = 0; i < array.length; ++i) {
        await semaphore.acquire(); // Acquires a slot from the semaphore if available

        try {
            result = accumulatorFn(result, array[i]); // Reduce operation

            config.onProgress && config.onProgress({
                completed: i + 1, 
                currentValue: result,
            });
        } catch (error) {
            console.log('Error occurred during reduction', error);
        } finally {
            semaphore.release(); // Releases the slot back to the semaphore
        }

        if (i < array.length - 1) {
            await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, config.throttlingInterval));
        }
    }

    return result;
}
```
In this code, `Semaphore` is a hypothetical class that implements semaphores for JavaScript (not actually built-in). If you want to use throttled concurrency in JavaScript, libraries like async or lodash provide such functionalities. 

