Here is a basic implementation of your request. Note that this doesn't include the database integration or parallel processing part as those would require specific implementations based on the used database and its API.

```javascript
function asyncReduce(array, initialValue, callback, { maxConcurrency = 10 } = {}) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    let result = initialValue;
    const length = array.length;

    if (length === 0) {
      resolve(result);
      return;
    }

    let pending = 0;
    let index = 0;
    function next() {
      ++pending;

      while (index < length && pending < maxConcurrency) {
        const currentIndex = index;
        ++index;

        Promise.resolve(array[currentIndex]).then((value, error) => {
          if (error || typeof value !== 'number') {
            // Skip non-numeric values
            next();
            return;
          }

          try {
            result = callback(result, value);
          } catch (e) {
            reject(e);
          }

          if (--pending === 0 && index === length) {
            resolve(result);
          } else {
            next(); // Process the next element
          }
        });
      }
    }

    next(); // Start processing
  });
}
```
This function processes elements in parallel, up to a maximum concurrency level defined by `maxConcurrency` (defaults to 10). Each processed value is passed to the callback function. If any errors occur during processing, they are handled and rejected with a corresponding error. The function also gracefully handles non-numeric values in the array by skipping them.

This implementation doesn't include edge case handling for very large numbers because JavaScript numbers can have any magnitude (within limits), unlike some other languages which might not support that. 

