```javascript
function findBooksInText(inputString, booksToIgnore = []) {
    // Precompile all regular expressions to improve performance
    const bookMatchers = Array.from({ length: 66 }, (_, i) => new RegExp(`\\b${books[i]}\\b`, 'gi'));

    let booksFound = {};

    if (typeof inputString !== 'string' || inputString === "") {
        throw new Error("Input must be a non-empty string.");
    }

    for (let matcher of bookMatchers) {
        const matchArray = Array.from(inputString.matchAll(matcher), m => m[0]);

        if (matchArray && matchArray.length > 0) {
            // Filter out books to ignore, and count occurrences in inputString
            matchArray.filter(book => !booksToIgnore.includes(book))
                      .forEach(book => booksFound[book] = booksFound[book] ? ++booksFound[book] : 1);
        }
    }

    if (Object.keys(booksFound).length === 0) {
        return "No books found in the text.";
    } else {
        return booksFound;
    }
}
```
This function uses a precompiled array of regular expressions, one for each book name from the Bible. It then loops through this array and uses `matchAll` to find all matches of each book's name in the input string, which is converted into an Array. The matches are filtered based on whether they should be ignored (i.e., if they exist in the booksToIgnore array) before being counted and stored in a booksFound object. If no books are found after filtering, the function returns a corresponding message.

Please note that you would need to define `books` array containing all book names from the Bible or Old Testament for this solution to work correctly.

