Here is an implementation that doesn't actually download data from URLs or handle JSON/XML parsing due to its complexity, but it should give you an idea on how such a function might look like in Rust with `tokio` for asynchronous tasks and `reqwest` for http requests. Note that this code has many simplifications and is just an example of what could be done:

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::time::Duration;
use tokio::task::JoinHandle;
use reqwest::Url;
use env_logger;

fn main() {
    pretty_env_logger::init();
}

async fn fetch_and_process_data(urls: Vec<String>) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let _ = tokio::spawn(async move {
        let client = reqwest::Client::new();

        for url in urls.into_iter() {
            let client = client.clone();
            let handle = tokio::spawn(fetch_url(&client, &url));
            handle.forget();
        }
    });

    Ok(())
}

async fn fetch_url(client: &reqwest::Client, url: &String) {
    let retry_interval = Duration::from_secs(1);

    for i in 0..3 {
        if let Err(e) = fetch_data(client, url).await {
            println_error(&format_args![
                "Failed to download data from {}, attempt: {}, err: {}", url, i + 1, e
            ]);

            tokio::time::sleep(retry_interval).await;
            retry_interval *= 2;
        } else {
            break;
        }
    }
}

async fn fetch_data(client: &reqwest::Client, url: &String) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let start = std::time::Instant::now();

    let response = client.get(url).send().await?;
    let status = response.status();

    match status.is_success() {
        true => println_info(&format_args![
            "Successfully downloaded data from {}, time taken: {} ms", 
            url, start.elapsed().as_millis()
        ]),
        false => return Err(Box::new(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::Other, format_args![
                "Failed to download data from {}, status: {}", url, status
            ])
        )),
    }

    // parse json, xml or csv here...

    Ok(())
}
```
This code simply uses the `reqwest` library to fetch some URLs in parallel. If fetching a URL fails, it waits for an exponentially increasing time before trying again (up to three times). 

It also logs information about successful and failed attempts using `env_logger` and `pretty_env_logger` libraries. The function `fetch_and_process_data()` is the main entry point of our code, it takes in a vector of URLs to fetch data from. Note that this will likely need more robust error handling and parsing of the downloaded content for JSON/XML/CSV format.

