Here is a high-level implementation of the service pool management in Java. This does not include all the requirements like Kafka integration, command line interface etc. You need to install Apache Kafka, MySQL server and set up SSL/TLS for secure communication with Kafka and MySQL respectively. 

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class ServicePoolManager {
    private static final Logger LOGGER = Logger.getLogger(ServicePoolManager.class.getName());
    private final BlockingQueue<Message> messageQueue = new LinkedBlockingDeque<>();
    private final ExecutorService executorService;
    private final List<Future> serviceFutures = new ArrayList<>();
    private final ScheduledExecutorService scheduledExecutorService = 
        Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());
    private final Random random = new Random();

    public ServicePoolManager(int poolSize) {
        this.executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(poolSize);
    }

    class Message {} // define message object
    interface IService {} // define service interface

    void startServices() {
        for (IService service : services) {
            final Future<?> future = 
                executorService.submit(() -> {
                    while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
                        try {
                            Message message = messageQueue.take();
                            service.handle(message);
                        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                        }
                    }
                });
            serviceFutures.add(future);
        }
    }

    void stopServices() {
        for (Future<?> future : serviceFutures) {
            future.cancel(true);
        }
    }

    private class Service implements IService {
        private final int maxMessages;

        public Service(int maxMessages) {
            this.maxMessages = maxMessages;
        }

        @Override
        public void handle(Message message) {
            // process the message
            int randomDelay = random.nextInt(1000); 
            try {
                Thread.sleep(randomDelay);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                LOGGER.severe("Service interrupted while processing a message: " + e);
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a pool of services that concurrently process messages from a queue with random delays between each operation. You would need to define your own `Message` and `Service` implementations for this to work properly in your application. Also, the provided example does not cover all features such as monitoring, configuration management, error handling, logging and user interface you have mentioned in requirements.

